function Gallery({ id, setId }) {
  const open = (next) => {
    document.startViewTransition(() => {
      setId(next);
    });
  };

  return <Grid current={id} onSelect={open} />;
}

referenceAnswer:
startViewTransition captures the 'after' state as soon as the callback (or the promise it returns) settles, but a React setState is asynchronous, so at that moment the DOM is still the old one. The direct fix is to force the commit inside the callback:

```jsx
import { flushSync } from 'react-dom';

document.startViewTransition(() => {
  flushSync(() => setId(next));
});
```

flushSync comes from react-dom, not from react, and forces a synchronous re-render so the new DOM is in place before the second snapshot. On React 19.3 or newer (stable since September 2026) you can instead let React drive the transition:

```jsx
<ViewTransition>
  <Grid current={id} />
</ViewTransition>
// and update inside a transition:
startTransition(() => setId(next));
```

ViewTransition animates updates made inside startTransition, a useDeferredValue change or a Suspense reveal — a plain setState stays urgent and does not animate.

